<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazi Nazrul Islam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nazrul Islam, Nazrul, Rebel Poet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazi Nazrul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was a Bengali poet, writer, journalist and musician, later honoured as the national poet of Bangladesh. Born in Churulia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Presidency, he worked as a muezzin and with a travelling folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> group as a young man, and served in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> from 1917. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> his poem Bidrohi (The Rebel, 1922) earned him the name Rebel Poet, and his bi-weekly Dhumketu and other writings against British rule led to repeated imprisonment. He wrote and composed nearly 4,000 songs, known as Nazrul Gīti, brought the ghazal into Bengali and opposed religious, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and gender bigotry. Ill from 1942, he was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> in 1972 and died there in 1976.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazi-nazrul-islam.jpg" alt="Kazi Nazrul Islam" width="330" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nazrul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the coup of 15 August 1975, President Khondaker Mostaq Ahmed set up a committee to replace it, which recommended <b>Kazi Nazrul Islam</b>'s &quot;Notuner Gaan&quot; (today the national march) and Farrukh Ahmed's &quot;Panjeri&quot;; the process stopped when he was removed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li><li><blockquote>In Bengal the poet <b>Kazi Nazrul Islam</b>, who is thought to have learned the melody while fighting in the Middle East in World War I, set it in the 1930s to an Islamic song about Muhammad and to songs of youth and of the monsoon rain.</blockquote><a href="/songs/katibim">Kâtibim (Üsküdar'a Gider İken)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kazi Nazrul Islam</b> translated it into Bengali around 1927, Ho Chi Minh into Vietnamese, Abolqasem Lahouti into Persian, Yeghishe Charents into Armenian; it was sung by workers in South Africa until the Communist Party was suppressed from 1950, and returned on South African radio in Zulu in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Churulia, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.78,87.08&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Churulia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6870 1287 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kazi Nazrul Islam"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7320 1557h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7320 1557h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.78,87.08&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
